I've added §11 to Design.md with Phase 1 broken into two parallel tracks:

Track A — NATS/JetStream Infrastructure (A1–A7): pin tooling → define an adapter identity manifest (the one source of truth for who's allowed to say what) → bootstrap script for Operator/Account/User JWTs → nats-server.conf → bootstrap the EVENTS stream + service-directory KV bucket → infra-only docker-compose → manual smoke test with the nats CLI.

Track B — gsb-core library (B1–B7): package scaffold → envelope models → crypto (encryption + signing) → config loader → registry client → BusClient façade → an end-to-end proof test (two fake adapters exchanging a signed+encrypted event).

B1–B4 are pure logic and can start immediately, in parallel with Track A. B5–B7 need a running, bootstrapped NATS (A6) to build against. A7 and B7 together are the exit criteria for Phase 1 — once both pass, Phase 2's vertical slice can begin.

I also surfaced four implementation parameters this breakdown assumes (KV TTL = 60s, heartbeat = 20s, memory-based JWT resolver, and — importantly — that nsc key material is never committed, only regenerated via a bootstrap script) with rationale, in case any should change before you start on this.

